\title{The Cost--Reliability Trade-off for Windowed Min-Plus Decoders}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{18 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We give a complete two-sided analysis of the trade-off between computational cost and
failure probability for windowed min-plus (tropical) decoders on a chain, and we prove
that the natural split of the analysis into an algebraic and a probabilistic half is
\emph{necessary}. On the algebraic side we study the min-plus action
$(A\otimes v)_a=\min_b(A_{ab}+v_b)$ of a transfer matrix on cost-to-go vectors, measured
in the span seminorm $\spn(v)=\max_a v_a-\min_a v_a$, the projective quantity a min-plus
decoder perceives. A tropically stochastic step is nonexpansive for $\spn$, and
\emph{every} step satisfies the tropical Dobrushin bound $\spn(A\otimes v)\le\Delta(A)$
with $\Delta(A)=\max_{a,a',b}(A_{ab}-A_{a'b})$, uniformly in $v$. Chaining yields an
absorption theorem: after a window of $k$ steps the span is at most
$\min_{j<k}\Delta(A^{(i+j)})$, so a single informative step anywhere in the window
suffices. We then show this is the end of the algebraic road: on a two-state chain of
diameter $d$ the span equals $d$ exactly after every number of steps, so no bound
$\spn\le\rho^{k}\spn(v)$ with $\rho<1$ can hold. On the probabilistic side, modelling
informativeness as a Bernoulli($p$) product measure, the window-$b$ failure probability
satisfies $\tfrac12\lfloor n/b\rfloor(1-p)^b\le\Prp[\mathcal F_{n,b}]\le(n+1-b)(1-p)^b$,
the lower bound by a second-order Bonferroni inequality on disjoint windows. With cost
$C(b)=nbq^{2}$ we obtain the invariant
$\log\bigl(1/\Prp[\mathcal F_{n,b}]\bigr)\,nq^{2}\le C(b)\log\frac1{1-p}$, a converse
forcing $b\ge\log(1/\varepsilon)/\log\frac1{1-p}$, and a matching achievability threshold
$b\ge(\log n+\log(1/\varepsilon))/\log\frac1{1-p}$.
\end{abstract}

